//? Title: Dropout: Moments, Implicit Ridge, Self-Normalisation and Monte Carlo Uncertainty
//? Subtitle: Inverted dropout's statistics and its train and inference modes; why dropout on a linear model is a ridge penalty scaled by each feature's energy; Gaussian, alpha, spatial and drop-path variants; what plain and alpha dropout do to a self-normalising network; Monte Carlo dropout as an uncertainty estimate, and where it fails; and dropout on a small digits training set, using datamunge's nn::dropout, nn::gaussian_dropout, nn::alpha_dropout, nn::spatial_dropout and nn::drop_path
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Dropout zeroes each unit with probability \(p\) while training and rescales the survivors by \(1/(1-p)\). This report checks datamunge's dropout and four new variants against their defining moments. It derives the exact expected loss of
dropout on a linear model: least squares plus a ridge penalty that weights each coefficient by its feature's energy. Monte Carlo over 20000 masks agrees with it to within 1.4 standard errors, and SGD with dropout converges to the
closed-form minimiser to within 0.003. Plain dropout moves a 32-layer SELU network's fixed point from variance 1 to the predicted 1.63, while alpha dropout keeps it at 1. On a small regression, dropout cuts the test error by a factor of 7 to 11. Monte
Carlo dropout gives an uncertainty band that widens away from the data, but the band misses the true function at every grid point beyond the data. On a 300-image digits training set, dropout barely changes accuracy and worsens the inference-mode loss, while averaging 100 stochastic passes gives the best test loss of all,
0.41 against 0.57 without dropout.
)

\toc

> The problem

Dropout (Hinton et al., 2012; Srivastava et al., 2014) is a regulariser for neural networks: while training, each hidden unit is removed at random with probability \(p\). Its usual justifications are informal. It stops units from
co-adapting, or it averages an exponential number of thinned networks. Both are hard to check. Some of its effects can be made exact, though. For a linear model it is a specific penalty on the weights. In a deep network it changes the variance that
the signal settles to, which is what self-normalising networks rely on. Run at test time, it gives a distribution of predictions that is widely used as an uncertainty estimate.

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.2_reusable_layer_library/dropout.cpp`. That example checked the mask statistics and the inference identity, fitted a small network to 40 noisy points with and without dropout, and used
Monte Carlo dropout for a predictive spread. All three are kept here, with the theory behind each and four variants added to the library.

> The method

>> Inverted dropout and its variants

With keep probability \(k=1-p\) and a mask \(m\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(k)\) drawn independently for every element, inverted dropout computes \(y=x\,m/k\) while training and \(y=x\) at inference. Then

$$
\mathbb E[y]=x,\qquad \operatorname{Var}[y]=x^2\,\frac{p}{1-p},
$$
\alttext(The expected value of y is x, and the variance of y is x squared times p over 1 minus p.)

and \(y\) takes only the values 0 and \(x/k\). Four variants are new in datamunge. `nn::gaussian_dropout` multiplies by \(\xi\sim\mathcal N(1,p/(1-p))\), which has the same mean and variance as \(m/k\) (Srivastava et al., 2014). `nn::spatial_dropout` shares one mask
over all positions of a channel, for feature maps whose neighbouring values are nearly equal (Tompson et al., 2015). `nn::drop_path` shares one mask over all of an example, to skip a residual branch for that example (stochastic depth; Huang et al., 2016).

`nn::alpha_dropout` is for self-normalising networks (Klambauer et al., 2017). Their SELU activation \(f(z)=\lambda z\) for \(z>0\) and \(\lambda\alpha(e^z-1)\) otherwise, with LeCun-normal weights, drives every layer's pre-activations towards mean 0 and variance 1. Setting
units to 0 breaks this, because 0 is not a typical SELU output. Alpha dropout sets dropped units to the saturation value \(\alpha'=-\lambda\alpha\approx-1.7581\) instead and then applies \(y=a\,u+b\) to \(u=x\,m+\alpha'(1-m)\). For an input with mean 0 and variance 1,
\(\mathbb E[u]=p\alpha'\) and \(\operatorname{Var}[u]=k+p\alpha'^2-p^2\alpha'^2=k(1+p\alpha'^2)\), so

$$
a=\frac1{\sqrt{k\,(1+p\,\alpha'^2)}},\qquad b=-a\,p\,\alpha'
$$
\alttext(a equals 1 over the square root of k times 1 plus p alpha prime squared, and b equals minus a times p times alpha prime.)

restore mean 0 and variance 1.

>> Dropout on a linear model is a ridge penalty

Take least squares with dropout on the inputs: \(L(w)=\frac1n\|y-\tilde Xw\|^2\) with \(\tilde X=X\odot M/k\). For row \(i\), \(\tilde x_i\cdot w\) has mean \(x_i\cdot w\) and variance \(\frac pk\sum_jx_{ij}^2w_j^2\), since the masks are independent. The expected squared residual
is the squared mean residual plus this variance, so

$$
\mathbb E_M\,L(w)=\frac1n\Big(\|y-Xw\|^2+\frac{p}{1-p}\sum_jw_j^2\sum_ix_{ij}^2\Big),
$$
\alttext(The expected loss over the masks equals 1 over n, times the squared norm of y minus X w, plus p over 1 minus p times the sum over j of w j squared times the sum over i of x i j squared.)

minimised by \(w_p=\big(X^\top X+\lambda\operatorname{diag}(X^\top X)\big)^{-1}X^\top y\) with \(\lambda=p/(1-p)\) (Wager, Wang and Liang, 2013). This is ridge regression with each coefficient's penalty scaled by its feature's energy \(\sum_ix_{ij}^2\), so rescaling a
feature does not change the fit. Gaussian dropout has the same first two moments, and the expected loss of a linear model depends on nothing else, so its expected loss is the same. Each SGD step with a fresh mask uses an unbiased gradient of
\(\mathbb E_ML\), so SGD with a decaying step size converges to \(w_p\). Averaging the iterates (Polyak and Juditsky, 1992) removes most of the remaining noise.

>> Dropout in a self-normalising network

Each layer of a wide network with LeCun-normal weights (\(nv_w=1\)) maps the pre-activation variance \(q\) to the mean of the squared input of the next layer. That gives three recursions,

$$
q\mapsto\mathbb E[f(Z)^2],\qquad q\mapsto\frac{\mathbb E[f(Z)^2]}{k},\qquad q\mapsto a^2\big(k\,\mathbb E[f(Z)^2]+p\alpha'^2-(k\mu+p\alpha')^2\big)+(ak\mu)^2,
$$
\alttext(Without dropout q maps to the expected value of f of Z squared. With dropout it maps to that divided by k. With alpha dropout it maps to a squared times k times the expected value of f of Z squared plus p alpha prime squared minus k mu plus p alpha prime all squared, plus a k mu squared.)

for no dropout, plain dropout and alpha dropout, with \(Z\sim\mathcal N(0,q)\) and \(\mu=\mathbb E f(Z)\) (computed with `nn::activation_moments`). SELU makes \(q=1\) a fixed point of the first map, and the alpha-dropout map keeps it. Plain dropout divides by \(k\), which moves the fixed point. At inference the network then sees variances
it was not trained on.

>> Monte Carlo dropout

Keeping dropout on at test time and averaging \(T\) stochastic forward passes gives a predictive mean and spread. Gal and Ghahramani (2016) interpret this as approximate Bayesian inference, in which the masks sample from an approximate posterior over the weights.
The spread measures disagreement among thinned networks. It is not guaranteed to cover the truth, and it need not shrink as data are added (Osband, 2016). For classification, averaging the predicted probabilities over passes gives a
smoother predictive distribution than one pass with dropout off.

> Simple examples

>> Mask statistics and the variants

The program repeats the original example's statistics on 100000 units of \(x=1\) for five rates, and checks that inference mode is the identity. It then measures the moments of the variants at rate 0.2, and checks that the spatial and drop-path masks are shared as intended.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

int main() {
  nn::Generator gen(7);
  const std::int64_t n = 100000;
  // 1. Inverted dropout on x = 1: fraction dropped, mean, variance against p / (1 − p), and the values taken.
  std::print("| rate p | share of zeros | mean | variance | p/(1 − p) | values |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  const Tensor one = nn::ones({n}, F64);
  for (double p : {0.0, 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 0.8}) {
    const Tensor y = nn::dropout(one, p, true, gen);
    const double zeros = nn::mean(nn::cast(nn::equal(y, 0.0), F64)).item(), m = nn::mean(y).item();
    const double v = nn::mean(nn::square(y - m)).item();
    std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {{0, {:.4g}}} |\n", p, zeros, m, v, p / (1 - p), nn::max(y).item());
  }
  const Tensor xr = nn::normal({n}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
  std::print("\nInference (training = false), p = 0.5: {} of {} outputs identical to the input.\n",
             static_cast<std::int64_t>(nn::sum(nn::cast(nn::equal(nn::dropout(xr, 0.5, false, gen), xr), F64)).item()), n);

  // 2. The variants at rate 0.2: Gaussian dropout on x = 1, and alpha dropout on standard normal inputs
  //    (plain dropout shown for comparison).
  std::print("\n| variant, p = 0.2 | input | mean | variance | target mean, variance |\n| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  auto moments = [](const Tensor& y) {
    const double m = nn::mean(y).item();
    return std::pair{m, nn::mean(nn::square(y - m)).item()};
  };
  {
    const auto [m, v] = moments(nn::gaussian_dropout(one, 0.2, true, gen));
    std::print("| gaussian_dropout | 1 | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | 1, 0.25 (as dropout) |\n", m, v);
    const auto [m2, v2] = moments(nn::dropout(xr, 0.2, true, gen));
    std::print("| dropout | N(0, 1) | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | 0, 1.25 |\n", m2, v2);
    const auto [m3, v3] = moments(nn::alpha_dropout(xr, 0.2, true, gen));
    std::print("| alpha_dropout | N(0, 1) | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | 0, 1 |\n", m3, v3);
    // SELU outputs of N(0, 1) inputs have mean ≈ 0 and variance ≈ 1 at the fixed point; alpha dropout keeps that.
    const Tensor s = nn::selu(xr);
    const auto [m4, v4] = moments(s);
    const auto [m5, v5] = moments(nn::alpha_dropout(s, 0.2, true, gen));
    std::print("| alpha_dropout | selu(N(0, 1)): mean {:.4f}, variance {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | unchanged for mean 0, variance 1 |\n", m4, v4, m5, v5);
  }

  // 3. Mask sharing: spatial dropout on ones [4, 6, 6, 8] and drop path on ones [8, 6, 6, 4].
  {
    const Tensor s = nn::spatial_dropout(nn::ones({4, 6, 6, 8}, F64), 0.5, true, gen);
    int constant = 0, kept = 0;
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i)
      for (std::int64_t c = 0; c < 8; ++c) {
        const Tensor map = nn::slice(s, {i, 0, 0, c}, {1, 6, 6, 1});
        constant += nn::max(map).item() == nn::min(map).item();
        kept += nn::max(map).item() > 0;
      }
    const Tensor d = nn::drop_path(nn::ones({8, 6, 6, 4}, F64), 0.25, true, gen);
    int dconst = 0;
    std::string pattern;
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
      const Tensor ex = nn::slice(d, {i, 0, 0, 0}, {1, 6, 6, 4});
      dconst += nn::max(ex).item() == nn::min(ex).item();
      pattern += std::format("{}{:.4g}", i ? ", " : "", nn::max(ex).item());
    }
    std::print("\nspatial_dropout, p = 0.5, ones [4, 6, 6, 8]: {} of 32 (example, channel) maps constant over the 36 positions, {} kept (value 2).\n", constant, kept);
    std::print("drop_path, p = 0.25, ones [8, 6, 6, 4]: {} of 8 examples constant over their 144 entries; values per example: {}.\n", dconst, pattern);
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| rate p | share of zeros | mean | variance | p/(1 − p) | values |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| 0 | 0.0000 | 1.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | {0, 1} |
| 0.1 | 0.1005 | 0.9994 | 0.1116 | 0.1111 | {0, 1.111} |
| 0.25 | 0.2507 | 0.9991 | 0.3339 | 0.3333 | {0, 1.333} |
| 0.5 | 0.4994 | 1.0012 | 1.0000 | 1.0000 | {0, 2} |
| 0.8 | 0.8000 | 1.0001 | 4.0001 | 4.0000 | {0, 5} |

Inference (training = false), p = 0.5: 100000 of 100000 outputs identical to the input.

| variant, p = 0.2 | input | mean | variance | target mean, variance |
| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| gaussian_dropout | 1 | 0.9997 | 0.2500 | 1, 0.25 (as dropout) |
| dropout | N(0, 1) | -0.0063 | 1.2490 | 0, 1.25 |
| alpha_dropout | N(0, 1) | -0.0024 | 1.0015 | 0, 1 |
| alpha_dropout | selu(N(0, 1)): mean -0.0037, variance 0.9982 | -0.0046 | 0.9993 | unchanged for mean 0, variance 1 |

spatial_dropout, p = 0.5, ones [4, 6, 6, 8]: 32 of 32 (example, channel) maps constant over the 36 positions, 17 kept (value 2).
drop_path, p = 0.25, ones [8, 6, 6, 4]: 8 of 8 examples constant over their 144 entries; values per example: 0, 1.333, 1.333, 1.333, 1.333, 1.333, 0, 1.333.
// result_end

The share of zeros is within 0.0007 of \(p\) at every rate, the mean within 0.0012 of 1 and the variance within 0.0006 of \(p/(1-p)\). The only values are 0 and \(1/(1-p)\). In inference mode all 100000 outputs equal the input exactly.
Gaussian dropout has mean 0.9997 and variance 0.2500, the moments of the Bernoulli mask at \(p=0.2\). On standard normal inputs plain dropout raises the variance to 1.249, the predicted 1.25, while alpha dropout keeps it at 1.0015 with mean \(-0.0024\).
SELU applied to standard normal inputs gives mean \(-0.004\) and variance 0.998, close to its fixed point, and alpha dropout leaves these at \(-0.005\) and 0.999. All 32 spatial-dropout maps are constant over their 36 positions, with 17 kept at value 2. All 8 drop-path
examples are constant, two of them dropped and six scaled to \(4/3\).

> Checking the theory

>> Implicit ridge, the SELU fixed point and Monte Carlo dropout

The program builds a regression with 60 rows and 5 correlated columns of scales 1, 2, 0.5, 1 and 3. For a fixed random \(w\) it compares the closed-form expected loss with an average over 20000 masks, for Bernoulli and Gaussian dropout. It then runs 40000 steps of
full-batch SGD with a fresh mask each step, with step size \(0.004/(1+t/4000)\), and averages the second half of the iterates. Next comes a 32-layer SELU network of width 256 without bias, on 1024 standard normal inputs, with dropout at rate 0.1 after every
activation. Last, the program repeats the original regression: an MLP \(1\to256\to256\to1\) with ReLU, fitted by 1000 Adam steps in single precision to 40 points of \(\sin3x+\mathcal N(0,0.3^2)\) on \([-1,1]\), with dropout after both hidden layers and three seeds per rate. It runs 100 stochastic passes on a grid over \([-1.5,1.5]\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dropout_theory.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Activation;
using nn::Tensor;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;
using dm::graphics::RGB;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  nn::Generator gen(11);
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2);

  // 1. Linear regression with dropout on the inputs. n = 60 rows, d = 5 correlated columns of different scales.
  const std::int64_t n = 60, d = 5;
  const Tensor scales = nn::tensor({1.0, 2.0, 0.5, 1.0, 3.0}, {}, F64);
  const Tensor Z = nn::normal({n, d}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
  const Tensor X = (Z + nn::slice(Z, {0, 0}, {n, 1}) * 0.6) * scales;  // every column shares 0.6 of column 0
  const Tensor beta = nn::tensor({1.0, -1.0, 2.0, 0.5, 0.3}, {d, 1}, F64);
  const Tensor y = nn::matmul(X, beta) + nn::normal({n, 1}, 0, 0.5, gen, F64);
  const Tensor col_sq = nn::sum(nn::square(X), {0});  // Σ_i x_ij²
  auto expected_loss = [&](const Tensor& w, double p) {
    const double ols = nn::sum(nn::square(y - nn::matmul(X, w))).item();
    return (ols + p / (1 - p) * nn::sum(col_sq * nn::square(nn::reshape(w, {d}))).item()) / n;
  };
  auto ridge = [&](double p) {  // (XᵀX + λ diag(XᵀX))⁻¹ Xᵀy, λ = p / (1 − p)
    const Tensor A = nn::matmul(X, X, true, false) + nn::matrix_diag(col_sq) * (p / (1 - p));
    return nn::solve(A, nn::matmul(X, y, true, false));
  };
  {
    const Tensor w = nn::normal({d, 1}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
    std::print("| loss of a fixed w under | Monte Carlo, 20000 masks (± standard error) | closed form | difference in standard errors |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (double p : {0.2, 0.5}) {
      for (bool gaussian : {false, true}) {
        double mc = 0, mc2 = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < 20000; ++r) {
          const Tensor Xd = gaussian ? nn::gaussian_dropout(X, p, true, gen) : nn::dropout(X, p, true, gen);
          const double l = nn::sum(nn::square(y - nn::matmul(Xd, w))).item() / n;
          mc += l / 20000, mc2 += l * l / 20000;
        }
        const double cf = expected_loss(w, p), se = std::sqrt((mc2 - mc * mc) / 20000);
        std::print("| {} dropout, p = {} | {:.4f} ± {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.1f} |\n", gaussian ? "Gaussian" : "Bernoulli", p, mc, se, cf, (mc - cf) / se);
      }
    }
  }
  // SGD with a fresh mask every step (full batch), decaying step size, averaging the second half of the iterates.
  std::print("\n| p | closed-form minimiser | SGD with dropout, averaged | max abs. difference | Gaussian dropout SGD, max abs. difference |\n| ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  auto vec_str = [](const Tensor& w) {
    std::string s = "(";
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < w.numel(); ++j) s += std::format("{}{:.3f}", j ? ", " : "", w.at({j, 0}));
    return s + ")";
  };
  std::vector<double> sgd_p;
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> sgd_w(d);
  for (double p : {0.2, 0.4, 0.6}) {
    const Tensor wr = ridge(p);
    double gdiff = 0;
    Tensor avg_b;
    for (bool gaussian : {false, true}) {
      Tensor w = nn::zeros({d, 1}, F64), avg = nn::zeros({d, 1}, F64);
      const int steps = 40000;
      for (int t = 0; t < steps; ++t) {
        const Tensor Xd = gaussian ? nn::gaussian_dropout(X, p, true, gen) : nn::dropout(X, p, true, gen);
        const Tensor g = nn::matmul(Xd, nn::matmul(Xd, w) - y, true, false) * (2.0 / n);
        w = w - g * (0.004 / (1 + t / 4000.0));
        if (t >= steps / 2) avg = avg + w / (steps / 2.0);
      }
      if (gaussian) gdiff = nn::max(nn::abs(avg - wr)).item();
      else avg_b = avg;
    }
    sgd_p.push_back(p);
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < d; ++j) sgd_w[j].push_back(avg_b.at({j, 0}));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n", p, vec_str(wr), vec_str(avg_b), nn::max(nn::abs(avg_b - wr)).item(), gdiff);
  }
  {
    const Tensor ols = ridge(0.0);
    std::print("Least squares (p = 0): {}; true β = {}.\n", vec_str(ols), vec_str(beta));
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    const std::vector<RGB> colours{{37, 99, 235}, {234, 88, 12}, {22, 163, 74}, {147, 51, 234}, {100, 116, 139}};
    std::vector<double> ps;
    for (int k = 0; k <= 40; ++k) ps.push_back(0.02 * k);
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < d; ++j) {
      std::vector<double> path;
      for (double p : ps) path.push_back(ridge(p).at({j, 0}));
      pl.lines(ps, path, std::format("w{} (scale {})", j + 1, scales.at({j})), colours[j], 2.2).points(sgd_p, sgd_w[j], "", colours[j], 5);
    }
    pl.x_limits(0, 0.8).y_limits(-1.6, 3.2).title("Dropout on a linear model = adaptive ridge").x_label("dropout rate p").y_label("coefficient");
    fig.add(pl);
  }

  // 2. A 32-layer SELU network (width 256, LeCun normal, no bias) on standard normal inputs: variance of each
  //    layer's pre-activations with no dropout, plain dropout and alpha dropout at p = 0.1 after every SELU,
  //    against the mean-field recursions.
  {
    const int depth = 32, width = 256;
    const double p = 0.1, k = 1 - p;
    const double ap = -1.0507009873554805 * 1.6732632423543772, a = 1 / std::sqrt(k * (1 + p * ap * ap));
    const Tensor x0 = nn::normal({1024, width}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
    std::vector<double> layers;
    for (int l = 1; l <= depth; ++l) layers.push_back(l);
    struct Run { std::string label; int kind; RGB colour; std::vector<double> var, theory; };
    std::vector<Run> runs = {{"no dropout", 0, {148, 163, 184}, {}, {}}, {"dropout", 1, {234, 88, 12}, {}, {}}, {"alpha dropout", 2, {37, 99, 235}, {}, {}}};
    nn::NoGradGuard ng;
    for (auto& r : runs) {
      nn::ParameterStore st(5, F64);
      nn::Generator dg(17);
      Tensor h = x0;
      double q = 1.0;  // pre-activation variance, mean field
      for (int l = 1; l <= depth; ++l) {
        const Tensor z = nn::dense(st, std::format("l{}", l), h, width, {.bias = false, .init = nn::init::lecun_normal()});
        r.var.push_back(nn::mean(nn::square(z)).item());
        r.theory.push_back(q);
        const Tensor s = nn::selu(z);
        h = r.kind == 0 ? s : r.kind == 1 ? nn::dropout(s, p, true, dg) : nn::alpha_dropout(s, p, true, dg);
        // Next pre-activation variance = E[h²] (n Var(w) = 1).
        const auto mom = nn::activation_moments(Activation::Selu, q);
        if (r.kind == 0) q = mom.second_moment;
        else if (r.kind == 1) q = mom.second_moment / k;
        else {
          const double mu = mom.mean, eu = k * mu + p * ap, eu2 = k * mom.second_moment + p * ap * ap;
          q = a * a * (eu2 - eu * eu) + (a * k * mu) * (a * k * mu);
        }
      }
      std::print("{}: pre-activation variance at layers 1, 8, 16, 32 = {:.3f}, {:.3f}, {:.3f}, {:.3f}; mean-field {:.3f}, {:.3f}, {:.3f}, {:.3f}.\n", r.label, r.var[0], r.var[7], r.var[15],
                 r.var[31], r.theory[0], r.theory[7], r.theory[15], r.theory[31]);
    }
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    for (const auto& r : runs) pl.lines(layers, r.theory, "", {30, 30, 30}, 1);
    for (const auto& r : runs) pl.points(layers, r.var, r.label, r.colour, 4.5);
    pl.x_limits(0, 33).y_limits(0.8, 2.0).title("SELU net, p = 0.1: variance of each layer").x_label("layer").y_label("pre-activation variance");
    fig.add(pl);
  }

  // 3. The original example's regression: an MLP 1-256-256-1 fit to 40 points of sin(3x) + N(0, 0.3²) on [−1, 1],
  //    1000 Adam steps in single precision, with dropout after each hidden layer; Monte Carlo dropout at test time (100 passes).
  std::mt19937_64 rng(42);
  std::uniform_real_distribution<double> U(-1, 1);
  std::normal_distribution<double> noise(0, 0.3);
  std::vector<double> xtr, ytr, xte;
  for (int i = 0; i < 40; ++i) {
    const double v = U(rng);
    xtr.push_back(v), ytr.push_back(std::sin(3 * v) + noise(rng));
  }
  for (int i = 0; i <= 200; ++i) xte.push_back(-1.5 + 3.0 * i / 200);
  const auto F32 = nn::DType::Float32;  // single precision keeps the twelve fits quick
  const Tensor Xtr = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(xtr, {40, 1}), F32), Ytr = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(ytr, {40, 1}), F32), Xte = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(xte, {201, 1}), F32);
  std::vector<double> xin, yin;
  for (double v : xte)
    if (std::abs(v) <= 1) xin.push_back(v), yin.push_back(std::sin(3 * v));
  const Tensor Xin = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(xin, {static_cast<std::int64_t>(xin.size()), 1}), F32), Yin = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(yin, {static_cast<std::int64_t>(yin.size()), 1}), F32);
  struct McFit { std::vector<double> mean, sd, pred; double train, test; };
  auto train = [&](double p, std::uint64_t seed) {
    nn::ParameterStore st(seed, F32);
    nn::Generator d1(seed * 10 + 1), d2(seed * 10 + 2);
    const nn::DenseOptions hid{.activation = Activation::Relu, .init = nn::init::he_uniform()};
    auto f = [&](const Tensor& x, bool training) {
      Tensor h = nn::dropout(nn::dense(st, "h1", x, 256, hid), p, training, d1);
      h = nn::dropout(nn::dense(st, "h2", h, 256, hid), p, training, d2);
      return nn::dense(st, "out", h, 1);
    };
    (void)f(Xtr, false);
    nn::Optimizer adam(st, 1e-3, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Adam});
    for (int s = 0; s < 1000; ++s) adam.minimize(nn::mse(f(Xtr, true), Ytr));
    nn::NoGradGuard ng;
    McFit r;
    r.train = nn::mse(f(Xtr, false), Ytr).item(), r.test = nn::mse(f(Xin, false), Yin).item();
    const Tensor yp = f(Xte, false);
    for (int i = 0; i < 201; ++i) r.pred.push_back(yp.at({i, 0}));
    if (p > 0) {
      std::vector<double> s1(201, 0), s2(201, 0);
      for (int k = 0; k < 100; ++k) {
        const Tensor yk = f(Xte, true);
        for (int i = 0; i < 201; ++i) s1[i] += yk.at({i, 0}) / 100, s2[i] += yk.at({i, 0}) * yk.at({i, 0}) / 100;
      }
      for (int i = 0; i < 201; ++i) r.mean.push_back(s1[i]), r.sd.push_back(std::sqrt(std::max(0.0, s2[i] - s1[i] * s1[i])));
    }
    return r;
  };
  std::print("\n| dropout rate | train MSE, seeds 1–3 | mean | test MSE on [−1, 1], seeds 1–3 | mean |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  std::map<double, McFit> first_seed;
  for (double p : {0.0, 0.1, 0.3, 0.5}) {
    std::vector<double> tr, te;
    for (std::uint64_t seed : {1, 2, 3}) {
      const McFit r = train(p, seed);
      tr.push_back(r.train), te.push_back(r.test);
      if (seed == 1) first_seed[p] = r;
    }
    std::print("| {} | {:.4f}, {:.4f}, {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f}, {:.4f}, {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n", p, tr[0], tr[1], tr[2], (tr[0] + tr[1] + tr[2]) / 3, te[0], te[1], te[2],
               (te[0] + te[1] + te[2]) / 3);
  }
  {
    const McFit& r = first_seed[0.5];
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    const double ylo = std::floor(std::ranges::min(first_seed[0.0].pred) - 0.3);
    std::print("Lowest value of the fit without dropout on the test grid: {:.2f} (axis from {}).\n", std::ranges::min(first_seed[0.0].pred), ylo);
    std::vector<double> bx, by;
    for (int i = 0; i <= 200; ++i) bx.push_back(xte[i]), by.push_back(r.mean[i] + 2 * r.sd[i]);
    for (int i = 200; i >= 0; --i) bx.push_back(xte[i]), by.push_back(r.mean[i] - 2 * r.sd[i]);
    std::vector<double> truth;
    for (double v : xte) truth.push_back(std::sin(3 * v));
    pl.polygon(bx, by, {191, 219, 254}).lines(xte, truth, "sin(3x)", {30, 30, 30}, 1.5).lines(xte, r.mean, "MC mean ± 2 sd", {37, 99, 235}, 2.5)
        .lines(xte, first_seed[0.0].pred, "no dropout", {148, 163, 184}, 2)
        .points(xtr, ytr, "training data", {234, 88, 12}, 4.5).x_limits(-1.5, 1.5).y_limits(ylo, 4).title("MC dropout, p = 0.5, 100 passes").x_label("x").y_label("y");
    fig.add(pl);
    std::print("MC dropout, p = 0.5, seed 1: predictive sd at x = −1.5, −1, 0, 1, 1.5: {:.3f}, {:.3f}, {:.3f}, {:.3f}, {:.3f}.\n", r.sd[0], r.sd[33], r.sd[100], r.sd[167], r.sd[200]);
    int covered = 0, outside = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i <= 200; ++i)
      if (std::abs(xte[i]) > 1) ++outside, covered += std::abs(std::sin(3 * xte[i]) - r.mean[i]) <= 2 * r.sd[i];
    std::print("Outside [−1, 1]: sin(3x) lies within the MC mean ± 2 sd at {} of {} grid points; at x = −1.5 the band is [{:.2f}, {:.2f}] and sin(−4.5) = {:.2f}.\n", covered, outside,
               r.mean[0] - 2 * r.sd[0], r.mean[0] + 2 * r.sd[0], std::sin(-4.5));
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    const std::vector<std::pair<double, RGB>> rates{{0.1, {22, 163, 74}}, {0.3, {234, 88, 12}}, {0.5, {37, 99, 235}}};
    for (const auto& [p, c] : rates) pl.lines(xte, first_seed[p].sd, std::format("p = {}", p), c, 2.5);
    pl.abline_v(-1, {148, 163, 184}).abline_v(1, {148, 163, 184}).x_limits(-1.5, 1.5).y_limits(0, 0.8).title("MC dropout predictive sd").x_label("x (data in [−1, 1])").y_label("sd over 100 passes");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 1000).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/dropout_theory.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
