\image(figures/dropout_theory.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Top left: the dropout-ridge coefficients against the dropout rate (lines) and the averaged SGD-with-dropout solutions at rates 0.2, 0.4 and 0.6 (points). Top right: the pre-activation variance of each layer of a 32-layer SELU network with no dropout, plain dropout and alpha dropout at rate 0.1, with the mean-field recursions as black lines. Bottom left: Monte Carlo dropout at rate 0.5, with the mean of 100 passes, a band of two standard deviations, the fit without dropout, the true function and the training points. Bottom right: the standard deviation over 100 passes for three rates; the data lie between the grey lines.)
\alttext(Top left: five smooth lines shrink towards zero as p grows from 0 to 0.8: w3 from 1.96 to 0.5, w1 from 0.96 to 0.2, w2 from minus 0.97 to minus 0.15, w4 and w5 slowly from about 0.5 and 0.3; the SGD points sit on the lines. Top right: the orange plain-dropout points rise from 1.0 at layer 1 to about 1.4 at layer 6 and level off near 1.6, along the black curve; the grey and blue points stay between 0.94 and 1.02 on the black line at 1. Bottom left: the blue mean curve follows the orange points inside [−1, 1] as a smoothed S and continues almost linearly outside, to about minus 1.5 on the left and 1.4 on the right, with a light blue band about 0.5 wide at the centre and 1.6 wide at the ends; the black sine curve leaves the band beyond the data; the grey no-dropout fit wiggles through the points with a spike down to minus 1.7 just left of 0. Bottom right: all three standard-deviation curves are lowest, 0.05 to 0.12, near x = 0 and rise towards both ends, the p = 0.5 curve highest at about 0.4 at x = ±1.5.)

The Monte Carlo averages of the dropout loss agree with the closed form to within 1.4 standard errors, for Bernoulli and Gaussian dropout at both rates. The averaged SGD solutions match the closed-form minimisers to within 0.002 for Bernoulli dropout and
0.003 for Gaussian dropout, at all three rates. As \(p\) grows every coefficient shrinks towards zero. From \(p=0\) to \(p=0.6\) they fall by between 32% (\(w_4\)) and 69% (\(w_2\)). Because each penalty is weighted by its feature's energy, the amount of shrinkage does not depend on the features' scales.

In the SELU network, plain dropout raises the pre-activation variance along the mean-field curve, to 1.595 at layer 32 against a predicted 1.625. With alpha dropout the variance stays at 0.98–1.00, and without dropout at 0.95–1.01, both close to the fixed point 1. At width 256 the per-layer
fluctuations amount to a few per cent.

Without dropout the network fits the 40 noisy points too closely: training error 0.056, below the noise variance 0.09, and test error 0.11. The figure shows why. The fit dips to \(-1.7\) between two close points just left of \(x=0\). Dropout raises the training error to and past the noise floor
and lowers the test error to 0.052 at \(p=0.1\), 0.055 at \(p=0.3\) and 0.081 at \(p=0.5\). The Monte Carlo standard deviation at \(p=0.5\) is 0.12 at \(x=0\), 0.30–0.33 at the edges of the data and 0.41 at \(x=\pm1.5\). It grows away from the data as intended, and
for all three rates. Outside \([-1,1]\), however, the true function lies outside the band of two standard deviations at all 68 grid points. At \(x=-1.5\) the band is \([-2.30,-0.68]\) while \(\sin(-4.5)=0.98\). The thinned networks agree with one another about a flat continuation, and the band
reflects that agreement.

> A dataset: dropout with 300 training images

The digits data (Alpaydin and Kaynak, 1998) has 1797 images of \(8\times8\) pixels. To make overfitting easy, the network \(64\to256\to256\to10\) with ReLU is trained on only the first 300 images and tested on the last 360. Training takes 1000 Adam steps in single precision at learning
rate 0.001 on batches of 32, about 107 passes over the data, with dropout after both hidden layers. The settings are no dropout, dropout at 0.2 and 0.5, and Gaussian dropout at 0.5, each with three seeds. The test loss is recorded every 50 steps, and at the end the
program also averages the predicted probabilities over 100 stochastic passes.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dropout_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
namespace init = dm::nn::init;
using nn::Activation;
using nn::Tensor;
const auto F32 = nn::DType::Float32;
using dm::graphics::RGB;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::int64_t n = 1797, d = 64, n_train = 300, n_test = 360;
  std::vector<double> v(static_cast<std::size_t>(n * d));
  for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < d; ++j) {
    const auto c = digits.get_column<double>(std::format("pixel_{}", j)).values();
    const double m = std::accumulate(c.begin(), c.end(), 0.0) / n / 16.0;
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) v[i * d + j] = c[i] / 16.0 - m;
  }
  const Tensor X = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(v, {n, d}), F32);  // single precision keeps the twelve fits quick
  const Tensor y = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(digits.get_column<double>("digit").values(), {n}), nn::DType::Int64);
  // A small training set (the first 300 images) so that a 64-256-256-10 network can overfit; the last 360 images for testing.
  const Tensor Xtr = nn::slice(X, {0, 0}, {n_train, d}), ytr = nn::slice(y, {0}, {n_train});
  const Tensor Xte = nn::slice(X, {n - n_test, 0}, {n_test, d}), yte = nn::slice(y, {n - n_test}, {n_test});

  struct Setting { std::string label; int kind; double p; RGB colour; };  // kind: 0 dropout, 1 Gaussian dropout
  const std::vector<Setting> settings = {{"no dropout", 0, 0.0, {148, 163, 184}}, {"dropout, p = 0.2", 0, 0.2, {22, 163, 74}},
                                         {"dropout, p = 0.5", 0, 0.5, {37, 99, 235}}, {"Gaussian, p = 0.5", 1, 0.5, {234, 88, 12}}};
  const int steps = 1000, every = 50;
  std::vector<double> axis;
  for (int s = every; s <= steps; s += every) axis.push_back(s);
  std::vector<double> mc_nll_mean(4, 0.0);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> curve(settings.size(), std::vector<double>(axis.size(), 0.0));
  std::print("| setting | train accuracy | test accuracy | test NLL, inference mode | test accuracy, MC average of 100 passes | test NLL, MC average |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t si = 0; si < settings.size(); ++si) {
    const auto& st = settings[si];
    std::vector<double> tr_acc, te_acc, te_nll, mc_acc, mc_nll;
    double& mc_mean = mc_nll_mean[si];
    for (std::uint64_t seed : {1, 2, 3}) {
      nn::ParameterStore p(seed, F32);
      nn::Generator g1(seed * 10 + 1), g2(seed * 10 + 2);
      const nn::DenseOptions hid{.activation = Activation::Relu, .init = init::he_normal()};
      auto drop = [&](const Tensor& h, bool training, nn::Generator& g) {
        return st.kind == 0 ? nn::dropout(h, st.p, training, g) : nn::gaussian_dropout(h, st.p, training, g);
      };
      auto f = [&](const Tensor& x, bool training) {
        Tensor h = drop(nn::dense(p, "h1", x, 256, hid), training, g1);
        h = drop(nn::dense(p, "h2", h, 256, hid), training, g2);
        return nn::dense(p, "out", h, 10);
      };
      (void)f(nn::slice(Xtr, {0, 0}, {2, d}), false);
      nn::Optimizer opt(p, 0.001, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Adam});
      std::mt19937_64 rng(seed);
      std::vector<std::int64_t> order(n_train);
      std::iota(order.begin(), order.end(), 0);
      std::size_t pos = n_train;
      for (int s = 1; s <= steps; ++s) {
        if (pos + 32 > order.size()) std::shuffle(order.begin(), order.end(), rng), pos = 0;
        const Tensor idx = nn::from_vector(std::vector<std::int64_t>(order.begin() + pos, order.begin() + pos + 32), {32});
        pos += 32;
        opt.minimize(nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(f(nn::gather(Xtr, idx, 0), true), nn::gather(ytr, idx, 0)));
        if (s % every == 0) {
          nn::NoGradGuard ng;
          curve[si][s / every - 1] += nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(f(Xte, false), yte).item() / 3;
        }
      }
      nn::NoGradGuard ng;
      auto acc = [&](const Tensor& logits_or_probs, const Tensor& labels) {
        return nn::mean(nn::cast(nn::equal(nn::argmax(logits_or_probs, -1), labels), F32)).item();
      };
      tr_acc.push_back(acc(f(Xtr, false), ytr));
      const Tensor logits = f(Xte, false);
      te_acc.push_back(acc(logits, yte));
      te_nll.push_back(nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(logits, yte).item());
      if (st.p > 0) {
        Tensor probs = nn::zeros({n_test, 10}, F32);
        for (int k = 0; k < 100; ++k) probs = probs + nn::softmax(f(Xte, true), -1) / 100.0;
        mc_acc.push_back(acc(probs, yte));
        const Tensor picked = nn::sum(probs * nn::one_hot(yte, 10, 1.0, 0.0, F32), {1});
        mc_nll.push_back(-nn::mean(nn::log(picked)).item());
      }
    }
    auto fmt = [](const std::vector<double>& a, double scale, int prec) {
      if (a.empty()) return std::string("–");
      return std::format("{:.{}f} ({:.{}f}–{:.{}f})", scale * (a[0] + a[1] + a[2]) / 3, prec, scale * std::ranges::min(a), prec, scale * std::ranges::max(a), prec);
    };
    if (!mc_nll.empty()) mc_mean = (mc_nll[0] + mc_nll[1] + mc_nll[2]) / 3;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", st.label, fmt(tr_acc, 100, 1), fmt(te_acc, 100, 1), fmt(te_nll, 1, 3), fmt(mc_acc, 100, 1), fmt(mc_nll, 1, 3));
  }
  for (std::size_t si = 0; si < settings.size(); ++si) {
    const auto& c = curve[si];
    const auto best = std::ranges::min_element(c);
    std::print("{}: test NLL lowest {:.3f} at step {}, final {:.3f}.\n", settings[si].label, *best, axis[best - c.begin()], c.back());
  }
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for (std::size_t si = 0; si < settings.size(); ++si) plot.lines(axis, curve[si], settings[si].label, settings[si].colour, 2.5);
  plot.abline_h(mc_nll_mean[2], {37, 99, 235}, 1);
  plot.x_limits(0, steps).y_limits(0, 1.2).title("Digits, 300 training images: test loss during training (mean of 3 seeds)").x_label("Adam step (batches of 32)")
      .y_label("test cross-entropy, inference mode");
  plot.size(1100, 460).save("build/dropout_digits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| setting | train accuracy | test accuracy | test NLL, inference mode | test accuracy, MC average of 100 passes | test NLL, MC average |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| no dropout | 100.0 (100.0–100.0) | 88.1 (85.8–89.4) | 0.496 (0.454–0.575) | – | – |
| dropout, p = 0.2 | 100.0 (100.0–100.0) | 88.1 (85.8–89.7) | 0.522 (0.467–0.618) | 87.6 (85.6–89.2) | 0.452 (0.407–0.523) |
| dropout, p = 0.5 | 100.0 (100.0–100.0) | 88.8 (87.5–90.3) | 0.532 (0.492–0.604) | 88.2 (87.8–88.9) | 0.410 (0.387–0.454) |
| Gaussian, p = 0.5 | 100.0 (100.0–100.0) | 89.0 (87.2–90.3) | 0.531 (0.475–0.631) | 89.2 (87.8–90.0) | 0.412 (0.377–0.463) |
no dropout: test NLL lowest 0.428 at step 150, final 0.496.
dropout, p = 0.2: test NLL lowest 0.417 at step 150, final 0.522.
dropout, p = 0.5: test NLL lowest 0.412 at step 250, final 0.532.
Gaussian, p = 0.5: test NLL lowest 0.422 at step 250, final 0.531.
\image(figures/dropout_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The test cross-entropy in inference mode during training, the mean of three seeds, for four settings. The thin blue horizontal line is the final test cross-entropy of dropout at 0.5 when probabilities are averaged over 100 stochastic passes.)
\alttext(The grey and green curves start near 0.51 and 0.57 at step 50 and the blue and orange p = 0.5 curves near 0.78; all four reach their lowest values of 0.41 to 0.43 at steps 150 to 250 and then rise slowly together. The grey no-dropout curve ends lowest, at 0.50 at step 1000, and the other three end near 0.52 to 0.53. The thin blue horizontal line at 0.41 lies at or below every curve.)

Every network reaches 100% training accuracy. Test accuracy barely depends on dropout, from 88.1% without it to 89.0% for Gaussian dropout at \(p=0.5\), within the seed ranges. In inference mode the test loss is slightly worse with dropout: 0.50 without, 0.52 at \(p=0.2\) and 0.53 at \(p=0.5\). The training curves show the cause. Every
setting reaches its lowest test loss, 0.41–0.43, within the first 250 steps, and after that the networks grow more confident on the training images; dropout did not slow this here. Averaging probabilities over 100 stochastic passes changes the picture. The test loss falls to 0.45 at
\(p=0.2\), 0.41 at \(p=0.5\) and 0.41 for Gaussian dropout. At \(p=0.5\) this is as low as the best inference-mode value at any checkpoint, the early-stopping optimum (0.410 against 0.412 at step 250), and accuracy is about unchanged. Bernoulli and Gaussian dropout at the same rate behave alike, as their equal first two moments suggest.

> Conclusion

datamunge's dropout matches its definition: the right share of zeros, the right mean and variance, and an exact identity at inference. The four new variants have the moments and mask sharing they are designed for. For a linear model dropout
is a ridge penalty weighted by each feature's energy. The closed form agrees with Monte Carlo and with SGD, and Gaussian dropout gives the same penalty. In a deep SELU network plain dropout moves the stable variance from 1 to the 1.63 predicted by the mean-field
recursion, while alpha dropout keeps it at 1.

On a small regression, dropout cut the test error from 0.11 to 0.05–0.08 by stopping the network from threading through the noise. Monte Carlo dropout's spread grew away from the data, but it did not cover the true function there: it measures disagreement
between thinned networks, not distance from the truth. On 300 digits images dropout did not help accuracy and made the inference-mode loss worse. Averaging 100 stochastic passes, however, matched the best early-stopped test loss without having to stop early.

The library gained `nn::gaussian_dropout`, `nn::alpha_dropout`, `nn::spatial_dropout` and `nn::drop_path`, with tests.

> References

